that. Has there been a drop off

00:54:20.090 --> 00:54:21.958
in that? And I assume it has as

00:54:22.025 --> 00:54:24.094
well, offering those type of

00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:25.895
programs as well? Yeah, I, you

00:54:25.962 --> 00:54:26.529
know, thank you for bringing up

00:54:26.596 --> 00:54:28.331
that point, because we not only

00:54:28.398 --> 00:54:28.965
have focus group data that tell

00:54:29.032 --> 00:54:30.333
us that youth are interested in

00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:33.837
a range of activities. You know,

00:54:33.903 --> 00:54:34.371
show choir is one thing I

00:54:34.437 --> 00:54:35.972
mentioned, but yoga,

00:54:36.039 --> 00:54:37.841
interpretive dance and other

00:54:37.907 --> 00:54:39.876
forms of movement were also

00:54:39.943 --> 00:54:42.045
important. And we see a bit of a

00:54:42.112 --> 00:54:44.180
reverse gender gap, if you will.

00:54:44.247 --> 00:54:47.884
So we see many of our female

00:54:47.951 --> 00:54:49.953
youth having access to a

00:54:50.020 --> 00:54:51.888
diversity of forms of movement,

00:54:51.955 --> 00:54:53.990
whereas our male youth are

00:54:54.057 --> 00:54:57.894
reporting higher rates of

00:54:57.961 --> 00:55:01.898
soccer, baseball, basketball. So

00:55:01.965 --> 00:55:04.000
that's a different twist on the

00:55:04.067 --> 00:55:05.835
I think how we may traditionally

00:55:05.902 --> 00:55:08.071
look at the gender gap. So are

00:55:08.138 --> 00:55:10.206
we exposing youth independent of

00:55:10.273 --> 00:55:12.142
gender and other dimensions of

00:55:12.208 --> 00:55:16.112
identity to a range of ways to,

00:55:16.179 --> 00:55:18.114
to play, to have fun, to move,

00:55:18.181 --> 00:55:22.085
to engage in, in sports? Again,

00:55:22.152 --> 00:55:24.187
thank you so much. Doctor Bali.

00:55:24.254 --> 00:55:31.928
Really grateful, and, we now

00:55:31.995 --> 00:55:34.030
invite Edgar Cabral from the

00:55:34.097 --> 00:55:36.032
Legislative Analyst's Office to

00:55:36.099 --> 00:55:36.599
provide a brief overview of

00:55:36.666 --> 00:55:39.836
state school funding as it

00:55:39.903 --> 00:55:40.503
relates to physical education in

00:55:40.570 --> 00:55:42.105
California and, Mr. Cabral, I

00:55:42.172 --> 00:55:44.274
guess we follow the pattern, you

00:55:44.340 --> 00:55:46.142
can take that seat there. Thank

00:55:46.209 --> 00:55:49.979
thank you again for coming down.

00:55:50.046 --> 00:56:02.125
Turned out. Thank you. You may

00:56:02.192 --> 00:56:06.096
proceed when ready, sir. Thank

00:56:06.162 --> 00:56:06.730
you, Mr. Chair. And members of

00:56:06.796 --> 00:56:10.100
the committee. I'm Edgar Cabral

00:56:10.166 --> 00:56:10.667
with the legislative Analyst

00:56:10.734 --> 00:56:12.068
office. Our office is a

00:56:12.135 --> 00:56:12.702
nonpartisan advisor to the state

00:56:12.769 --> 00:56:14.170
legislature. And so, as someone

00:56:14.237 --> 00:56:16.473
who is used to testifying in the

00:56:16.539 --> 00:56:18.308
Capitol on committees, it is,

00:56:18.374 --> 00:56:21.945
for me, the opposite. Weird to

00:56:22.011 --> 00:56:22.579
be, next to you and facing the

00:56:22.645 --> 00:56:24.314
audience, but I think I could

00:56:24.380 --> 00:56:26.316
all do in a locker room. Yeah.

00:56:26.382 --> 00:56:28.151
Yes. In a locker room. Edgar is

00:56:28.218 --> 00:56:28.785
used to having his back to the

00:56:28.852 --> 00:56:33.957
audience. So, but, I feel like

00:56:34.023 --> 00:56:34.624
for me being rude to the members

00:56:34.691 --> 00:56:36.459
that I'm in, but you do fit in

00:56:36.526 --> 00:56:38.428
well here in this, this sports

00:56:38.495 --> 00:56:40.196
venue, thank you again. Thank

00:56:40.263 --> 00:56:42.031
you for having me. So, I was

00:56:42.098 --> 00:56:42.632
asked to just provide a brief

00:56:42.699 --> 00:56:44.367
overview of kind of the state's

00:56:44.434 --> 00:56:46.402
role in physical education. So

00:56:46.469 --> 00:56:48.171
as we talk about potentially

00:56:48.238 --> 00:56:48.805
solutions changes things that we

00:56:48.872 --> 00:56:52.142
can do to address some of the

00:56:52.208 --> 00:56:54.210
challenges that Doctor Bailey

00:56:54.277 --> 00:56:56.146
pointed out in from the survey

00:56:56.212 --> 00:56:58.114
that was conducted in the

00:56:58.181 --> 00:57:00.416
research. Just to get a sense of

00:57:00.483 --> 00:57:02.452
where are we now? What are the

00:57:02.519 --> 00:57:04.187
current rules and requirements

00:57:04.254 --> 00:57:06.222
and what does the state do in

00:57:06.289 --> 00:57:08.258
funding? So I have a very brief

00:57:08.324 --> 00:57:08.892
handout. There are a few copies

00:57:08.958 --> 00:57:10.426
of it. On the in the back, but

00:57:10.493 --> 00:57:12.362
also it's up on our website if

00:57:12.428 --> 00:57:14.097
anybody wants to look at it,

00:57:14.164 --> 00:57:16.065
it's our website is la Dot

00:57:16.132 --> 00:57:18.034
Ca.gov, and I'm just going to

00:57:18.101 --> 00:57:18.601
talk about three, three main

00:57:18.668 --> 00:57:20.370
areas where the state is

00:57:20.436 --> 00:57:22.172
involved. One is setting

00:57:22.238 --> 00:57:22.806
specific minimum requirements to

00:57:22.872 --> 00:57:25.975
Is funding, and then three is

00:57:26.042 --> 00:57:28.178
setting standards. So first when

00:57:28.244 --> 00:57:29.979
it comes to the minimum

00:57:30.046 --> 00:57:31.981
requirements just talk about

00:57:32.048 --> 00:57:33.983
what those are. So in statute

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:34.584
there is a requirement that we

00:57:34.651 --> 00:57:37.987
that there's a number of areas

00:57:38.054 --> 00:57:39.989
of study that are required to be

00:57:40.056 --> 00:57:40.623
provided in public schools. And

00:57:40.690 --> 00:57:42.325
physical education is one of

00:57:42.392 --> 00:57:45.995
those areas. And it's required

00:57:46.062 --> 00:57:48.164
for grades one through 12, the

00:57:48.231 --> 00:57:50.066
state also, and this is

00:57:50.133 --> 00:57:50.600
something that's unique to

00:57:50.667 --> 00:57:52.302
physical education versus other

00:57:52.368 --> 00:57:54.270
subject areas. There is a

00:57:54.337 --> 00:57:54.904
specific minute requirement in

00:57:54.971 --> 00:57:58.408
statute, and that requirement is

00:57:58.474 --> 00:58:02.078
for grades one through four, one

00:58:02.145 --> 00:58:02.612
through six, at least 200

00:58:02.679 --> 00:58:06.082
minutes for every ten school

00:58:06.149 --> 00:58:06.749
days. So about 20 minutes a day.

00:58:06.816 --> 00:58:08.585
And then in grades seven through

00:58:08.651 --> 00:58:10.286
12 to double that requirement.

00:58:10.353 --> 00:58:10.887
So it's 400 minutes for every

00:58:10.954 --> 00:58:14.057
ten school days. So about 40

00:58:14.123 --> 00:58:14.657
minutes a day, one thing I'll

00:58:14.724 --> 00:58:16.459
point out, because I think

00:58:16.526 --> 00:58:18.228
Doctor Bailey mentioned in the

00:58:18.294 --> 00:58:20.296
in, in their survey data, it's

00:58:20.363 --> 00:58:22.232
not every day this is an average

00:58:22.298 --> 00:58:24.434
over ten days. And so it could

00:58:24.500 --> 00:58:26.269
be that some school districts

00:58:26.336 --> 00:58:26.903
are lumping that activity in a

00:58:26.970 --> 00:58:28.371
couple of days, a week and not

00:58:28.438 --> 00:58:30.573
doing it every day. And so that,

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:32.442
that, that may mean that, you

00:58:32.508 --> 00:58:34.210
know, there are several days

00:58:34.277 --> 00:58:34.811
where there is no structured

00:58:34.878 --> 00:58:36.279
physical education in, in a

00:58:36.346 --> 00:58:38.081
student's day, I would also

00:58:38.147 --> 00:58:40.216
point out that these these

00:58:40.283 --> 00:58:42.085
minute requirements, this

00:58:42.151 --> 00:58:44.120
excludes recess or the lunch

00:58:44.187 --> 00:58:46.222
period. This is a time that's

00:58:46.289 --> 00:58:48.124
supposed to be in part of the

00:58:48.191 --> 00:58:50.093
school day, part of the

00:58:50.159 --> 00:58:50.593
instructional time of a

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:52.128
student's day, the next thing I

00:58:52.195 --> 00:58:54.330
would add is there's a

00:58:54.397 --> 00:58:54.964
graduation requirement, and that

00:58:55.031 --> 00:58:56.432
requirement is that students

00:58:56.499 --> 00:58:58.334
complete two year long courses

00:58:58.401 --> 00:59:00.503
in physical education to receive

00:59:00.570 --> 00:59:04.173
a high school diploma, again,

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:04.774
because it's only two out of

00:59:04.841 --> 00:59:08.144
those four years, it could be

00:59:08.211 --> 00:59:08.778
that there are some years where

00:59:08.845 --> 00:59:10.113
students do not enroll in a

00:59:10.179 --> 00:59:10.713
physical education course, and

00:59:10.780 --> 00:59:12.282
are not participating in that on

00:59:12.348 --> 00:59:14.117
a daily basis, the last thing

00:59:14.183 --> 00:59:16.586
I'll add that is a requirement

00:59:16.653 --> 00:59:18.321
at the state level is that

00:59:18.388 --> 00:59:18.988
public schools must administer a

00:59:19.055 --> 00:59:20.156
physical fitness test annually,

00:59:20.223 --> 00:59:20.723
and this is administered to

00:59:20.790 --> 00:59:22.458
students in grades five, seven

00:59:22.525 --> 00:59:26.396
and nine, and for those of you

00:59:26.462 --> 00:59:28.231
who went to school in

00:59:28.298 --> 00:59:28.865
California, I know, I remember,

00:59:28.932 --> 00:59:30.466
I remember at least a couple of

00:59:30.533 --> 00:59:32.268
these, and that includes

00:59:32.335 --> 00:59:34.270
measures of aerobic capacity,

00:59:34.337 --> 00:59:34.771
strength, endurance and

00:59:34.837 --> 00:59:38.274
flexibility, so for all this,

00:59:38.341 --> 00:59:40.143
there's a few more kind of

00:59:40.209 --> 00:59:40.777
detailed requirements, but that

00:59:40.843 --> 00:59:42.278
kind of gives you the basic

00:59:42.345 --> 00:59:42.879
framework. It's really about

00:59:42.946 --> 00:59:44.414
saying, public schools must

00:59:44.480 --> 00:59:46.382
provide physical education, and

00:59:46.449 --> 00:59:46.950
there's some specific minute

00:59:47.016 --> 00:59:48.518
requirements, the Department of

00:59:48.584 --> 00:59:52.455
Education does do monitoring of

00:59:52.522 --> 00:59:54.390
these requirements annually.

00:59:54.457 --> 00:59:54.991
They choose a small subset of

00:59:55.058 --> 00:59:58.227
school districts, and they do

00:59:58.294 --> 01:00:00.263
audits to monitor whether

01:00:00.330 --> 01:00:00.830
there's compliance with the

01:00:00.897 --> 01:00:02.265
requirements. And they do

01:00:02.332 --> 01:00:04.233
publish a report annually that's

01:00:04.300 --> 01:00:04.834
on their website that you can

01:00:04.901 --> 01:00:06.636
see where you can find. They

01:00:06.703 --> 01:00:08.338
show the number of schools in

01:00:08.404 --> 01:00:10.640
each district that they looked

01:00:10.707 --> 01:00:12.408
at, and they show if they did

01:00:12.475 --> 01:00:13.009
not meet those requirements,

01:00:13.076 --> 01:00:14.644
which of the requirements were

01:00:14.711 --> 01:00:16.512
not met. And so that's just a

01:00:16.579 --> 01:00:18.348
very quick high level on the

01:00:18.414 --> 01:00:20.550
requirements themselves. Next, I

01:00:20.616 --> 01:00:22.452
want to talk about funding. And

01:00:22.518 --> 01:00:24.253
so funding for physical

01:00:24.320 --> 01:00:26.222
education, like with essentially

01:00:26.289 --> 01:00:28.491
all subject areas in California,

01:00:28.558 --> 01:00:29.092
there's no specific earmarked

01:00:29.158 --> 01:00:30.626
funds for physical education.

01:00:30.693 --> 01:00:34.564
Instead schools are funded

01:00:34.630 --> 01:00:35.164
through the primarily through

01:00:35.231 --> 01:00:36.599
the local control Funding

01:00:36.666 --> 01:00:38.401
formula, which is the main

01:00:38.468 --> 01:00:40.269
funding source for schools in

01:00:40.336 --> 01:00:40.903
California. And that formula has

01:00:40.970 --> 01:00:44.240
a base level of funding that

01:00:44.307 --> 01:00:44.907
goes out to school districts and

01:00:44.974 --> 01:00:46.609
charter schools based on the

01:00:46.676 --> 01:00:48.311
number of students that are that

01:00:48.378 --> 01:00:48.911
are enrolled. And then there's

01:00:48.978 --> 01:00:52.382
also a component of the formula

01:00:52.448 --> 01:00:53.016
called the supplemental grant.

01:00:53.082 --> 01:00:54.584
And then the concentration grant

01:00:54.650 --> 01:00:58.388
that is, varies across across

01:00:58.454 --> 01:00:59.022
districts. That's based on the

01:00:59.088 --> 01:01:00.556
proportion of students in each

01:01:00.623 --> 01:01:02.492
district that are low income or

01:01:02.558 --> 01:01:04.494
English learners. So that those

01:01:04.560 --> 01:01:05.094
who have higher proportions of

01:01:05.161 --> 01:01:06.729
low income students or English

01:01:06.796 --> 01:01:08.464
learners get a little bit more

01:01:08.531 --> 01:01:10.366
funding, that that's the primary

01:01:10.433 --> 01:01:10.967
funding source for schools in

01:01:11.034 --> 01:01:12.368
California. That's where

01:01:12.435 --> 01:01:14.604
physical education activities

01:01:14.670 --> 01:01:15.238
are primarily being funded. And

01:01:15.304 --> 01:01:16.773
so that will include everything

01:01:16.839 --> 01:01:18.241
from, staff salaries and

01:01:18.307 --> 01:01:20.276
benefits, training,

01:01:20.343 --> 01:01:20.777
instructional materials,

01:01:20.843 --> 01:01:22.512
equipment that would be funded

01:01:22.578 --> 01:01:26.449
through Lcff, there are, and so

01:01:26.516 --> 01:01:30.253
I think, I think of that is that

01:01:30.319 --> 01:01:32.288
that's the funding source that's

01:01:32.355 --> 01:01:32.755
available to fund the

01:01:32.822 --> 01:01:33.356
instructional PE requirements,

01:01:33.423 --> 01:01:36.726
there are also two other large

01:01:36.793 --> 01:01:38.528
programs that the state has that

01:01:38.594 --> 01:01:42.265
are for before and after school.

01:01:42.331 --> 01:01:42.932
Funding that can also be used to

01:01:42.999 --> 01:01:44.534
complement the physical

01:01:44.600 --> 01:01:46.469
education instruction to provide

01:01:46.536 --> 01:01:48.771
more opportunities for physical

01:01:48.838 --> 01:01:50.339
activity throughout the day. One

01:01:50.406 --> 01:01:52.608
is known as the After School

01:01:52.675 --> 01:01:54.277
Education and Safety program,

01:01:54.343 --> 01:01:54.811
which is a long standing

01:01:54.877 --> 01:01:56.512
program. It's been around for

01:01:56.579 --> 01:01:58.381
more than 20 years. I think

01:01:58.448 --> 01:02:00.583
there's about $700 million in

01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:02.351
funding for that program, and

01:02:02.418 --> 01:02:04.320
that that funding goes out. The

01:02:04.387 --> 01:02:06.355
priority is for, school sites

01:02:06.422 --> 01:02:08.758
with higher proportions of low

01:02:08.825 --> 01:02:10.359
income students across the state

01:02:10.426 --> 01:02:11.027
that have access to these funds,

01:02:11.094 --> 01:02:14.397
there's also another larger

01:02:14.464 --> 01:02:16.566
program that was just created a

01:02:16.632 --> 01:02:17.133
few years ago, known as the

01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:18.267
Expanded Learning Opportunities

01:02:18.334 --> 01:02:20.303
Program. That program goes out

01:02:20.369 --> 01:02:22.271
to every school district and

01:02:22.338 --> 01:02:24.307
classroom based charter school

01:02:24.373 --> 01:02:24.941
in California. And that funding

01:02:25.007 --> 01:02:28.444
is provided and with the intent

01:02:28.511 --> 01:02:30.346
that there be, before and after

01:02:30.413 --> 01:02:30.947
school provided throughout the

01:02:31.013 --> 01:02:32.748
school year. And then also that

01:02:32.815 --> 01:02:34.550
school districts and charter

01:02:34.617 --> 01:02:36.552
schools offer 30 days of a nine

01:02:36.619 --> 01:02:40.356
hour programing in what we call

01:02:40.423 --> 01:02:40.957
intersession, which could be

01:02:41.023 --> 01:02:42.592
summer, but it could also be

01:02:42.658 --> 01:02:44.594
winter break. If there's a

01:02:44.660 --> 01:02:46.362
Thanksgiving break, days like

01:02:46.429 --> 01:02:48.264
that. So this is now a much

01:02:48.331 --> 01:02:48.898
broader, pot of money that goes

01:02:48.965 --> 01:02:52.502
out across the state that can be

01:02:52.568 --> 01:02:54.604
used to complement, other other

01:02:54.670 --> 01:02:56.539
programing and physical

01:02:56.606 --> 01:02:58.541
activities to promote physical

01:02:58.608 --> 01:03:00.643
activity. Now in terms of the

01:03:00.710 --> 01:03:01.244
requirements, the requirements

01:03:01.310 --> 01:03:02.812
are just that. There be some

01:03:02.879 --> 01:03:06.382
educational components in the in

01:03:06.449 --> 01:03:06.949
that program and then also

01:03:07.016 --> 01:03:08.484
enrichment components and that

01:03:08.551 --> 01:03:10.386
enrichment can include a variety

01:03:10.453 --> 01:03:12.688
of things. So a lot of times

01:03:12.755 --> 01:03:14.423
schools are offering, for

01:03:14.490 --> 01:03:15.057
example arts. Or they might be

01:03:15.124 --> 01:03:18.661
doing this where maybe there

01:03:18.728 --> 01:03:20.396
will be things like Stem

01:03:20.463 --> 01:03:20.997
programs like robotics, things

01:03:21.063 --> 01:03:21.597
like that. But sports is also

01:03:21.664 --> 01:03:22.698
one of the options. That's

01:03:22.765 --> 01:03:24.500
available for schools, I would

01:03:24.567 --> 01:03:28.404
also point out that I think,

01:03:28.471 --> 01:03:30.373
maybe touching back on some of

01:03:30.439 --> 01:03:30.973
this survey data that Doctor

01:03:31.040 --> 01:03:32.575
Bradley discussed about dropping

01:03:32.642 --> 01:03:36.412
off at, at kind of that teenage

01:03:36.479 --> 01:03:37.013
years, these before and after

01:03:37.079 --> 01:03:38.381
school programs, they're

01:03:38.447 --> 01:03:39.015
primarily in the elementary and

01:03:39.081 --> 01:03:40.583
middle school grades. So there's

01:03:40.650 --> 01:03:42.752
a lot less of these after school

01:03:42.818 --> 01:03:44.620
programs in, in the high school

01:03:44.687 --> 01:03:48.524
level. So finally, I just want

01:03:48.591 --> 01:03:50.726
to touch on, standards that the

01:03:50.793 --> 01:03:52.695
state state sets. There are two

01:03:52.762 --> 01:03:54.730
different things that the state

01:03:54.797 --> 01:03:56.566
does, and this applies to all

01:03:56.632 --> 01:03:57.200
subject areas, not just physical

01:03:57.266 --> 01:03:58.634
education. One is academic

01:03:58.701 --> 01:04:00.836
content standards. So the State

01:04:00.903 --> 01:04:02.672
Board of Education adopts

01:04:02.738 --> 01:04:03.339
content standards for every area

01:04:03.406 --> 01:04:06.375
of study. And these standards

01:04:06.442 --> 01:04:07.043
are usually developed with large

01:04:07.109 --> 01:04:10.513
groups of experts. They can be,

01:04:10.580 --> 01:04:12.615
teachers and people employed in

01:04:12.682 --> 01:04:14.717
in school districts, but also

01:04:14.784 --> 01:04:16.485
